%[text] %[text:anchor:T_DEF03274] # Numerical Methods with Applications
%[text] MATLAB R2024b or older: download the [legacy MLX release](https://github.com/MathWorks-Teaching-Resources/Numerical-Methods-with-Applications/releases/tag/mlx). This version uses plain-text live scripts and requires MATLAB R2025a or newer.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_053613DF] ## Information
%[text] This curriculum module contains interactive [MATLAB® live scripts](https://www.mathworks.com/products/matlab/live-editor.html) that teach fundamental concepts and basic terminology related to designing and implementing numerical methods related to interpolation, numerical integration and differentiation, and numerical solutions to ordinary and partial differential equations.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F00D98E4] ### Background
%[text] You can use these live scripts as demonstrations in lectures, class activities, or interactive assignments outside class. This module addresses standard topics from a first course in numerical methods including interpolation, numerical integration and derivation, and several numerical solution methods for ODEs and PDEs.
%[text] The instructions inside the live scripts will guide you through the exercises and activities. Get started with each live script by running it one section at a time. To stop running the script or a section midway (for example, when an animation is in progress), use the ![Stop icon](text:image:6427) Stop button in the **RUN** section of the **Live Editor** tab in the MATLAB Toolstrip.
%[text] ### Contact Us
%[text] Contact the [MathWorks Educator Content Development Team](mailto:onlineteaching@mathworks.com) if you would like to request assistance, provide feedback, or if you have a question.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_30BC7141] ### Prerequisites
%[text] This module assumes familiarity with basic programming concepts such as floating-point doubles and strings, structures including constants, vectors, matrices, arrays, control flows including if/else, for loops, and while loops, and how to use them in MATLAB. These ideas are all presented with interactive examples in [<u>Fundamentals of Programming</u>](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/103225-fundamentals-of-programming). This module assumes familiarity with polynomials, derivatives, and integrals. To use the partial differential equation scripts, a student must be familiar with the concepts of partial derivatives and multivariable functions. These scripts do not assume knowledge of analytic solutions to ordinary or partial differential equations.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_330E72C3] ### Getting Started
%[text] #### Accessing the Module
%[text] #### **On MATLAB Online:**
%[text] Use the [![Open in MATLAB Online Button](text:image:127a)](https://matlab.mathworks.com/open/github/v1?repo=MathWorks-Teaching-Resources/Numerical-Methods-with-Applications&project=NumericalMethods.prj) link to download the module. You will be prompted to log in or create a MathWorks account. The project will be loaded, and you will see an app with several navigation options to get you started.
%[text] #### **On Desktop:**
%[text] Download or clone this repository. Open MATLAB, navigate to the folder containing these scripts and double-click on [NumericalMethods.prj](<matlab: openProject("NumericalMethods.prj")>). It will add the appropriate files to your MATLAB path and open an app that asks you where you would like to start. 
%[text] Ensure you have all the required products ([listed below](internal:H_E850B4FF)) installed. If you need to include a product, add it using the Add-On Explorer. To install an add-on, go to the **Home** tab and select  ![Add-ons icon](text:image:94b6) **Add-Ons** \> **Get Add-Ons**. 
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E850B4FF] ### Products
%[text] MATLAB® is used throughout. Tools from the Symbolic Math Toolbox™ are used frequently as well. 
%[text] - `drawpolygon` from Image Processing and Computer Vision™ is used to create datasets in `Hand.m` and `MeasureLakeArea.m`
%[text] - Statistics and Machine Learning Toolbox™ and the Curve Fitting Toolbox™ are used in `Interpolation.m`
%[text] - `geoplayer` from Automated Driving Toolbox™ is used in TrackStorms.m to create elegant visualizations, but pedagogically similar plots are demonstrated with geoplot as well. \
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E8C62B23] ## Scripts
%[text] ### [**Interpolation.m**](file:./Scripts/Interpolation.m) 
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[-1,262,308],"ignoreHeader":true}
%[text] | ![An interpolated image of a hand](text:image:6390) | <p>**In this script, students will...**</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} define interpolation and explain how it is different from regression and extrapolation</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} demonstrate multiple solutions to an interpolation problem, even when interpolating by polynomials</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} implement three different interpolation functions</p><p></p> | <p>**Applications**</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} Drawing a smooth rendition of their hand</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} Tracking the path of a cyclone</p><p></p><p>**Scaffolded Template Scripts**</p><p>[`linInterp.m`](file:./FunctionLibrary/linInterp.m)</p><p>[`pcHInterp.m`](file:./FunctionLibrary/pcHInterp.m)</p><p>[`pcSpline.m`](file:./FunctionLibrary/pcSpline.m)</p> |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text:table]
%[text] ### Supporting Scripts
%[text] - [`Hand.m`](file:./Scripts/Hand.m)
%[text] - [`TrackStorms.m`](file:./Scripts/TrackStorms.m) \
%[text] ### [**NumericalDerivatives.m**](file:./Scripts/NumericalDerivatives.m) 
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[-1,262,308],"ignoreHeader":true}
%[text] | ![A minimally noisy dataset plotted together with its smooth theoretical and jagged numerical derivatives](text:image:5285) | <p>**In this script, students will...**</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} determine numerical derivative approximations formulas</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} use Taylor's theorem to calculate the order of the error for a numerical approximation to a derivative</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} demonstrate how numerical derivatives can magnify approximation errors</p> | <p>**Applications**</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} Numerical solutions to differential equations</p> |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text:table]
%[text] ### [**NumericalIntegration.m**](file:./Scripts/NumericalIntegration.m) 
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[-1,262,308],"ignoreHeader":true}
%[text] | ![Subdivision of an irregularly-shaped lake into rectangles](text:image:9a40) | <p>**In this script, students will...**</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} implement Euler's method, Gaussian 2-point approximations, and Simpson's rule for numerical integration</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} explain why higher-order approximations may not be appropriate in applications</p> | <p>**Applications**</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} Measure the area of a lake</p><p></p><p>**Scaffolded Template Scripts**</p><p>[`eulerMethod.m`](file:./FunctionLibrary/eulerMethod.m)</p><p>[`gauss2pt.m`](file:./FunctionLibrary/gauss2pt.m)</p><p>[`simpsonsRule.m`](file:./FunctionLibrary/simpsonsRule.m)</p> |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text:table]
%[text] ### Supporting Scripts
%[text] - [`MeasureLakeArea.m`](file:./Scripts/MeasureLakeArea.m) \
%[text] ### [**NumericalODEs.m**](file:./Scripts/NumericalODEs.m) 
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[-1,262,308],"ignoreHeader":true}
%[text] | ![A diagram of a pendulum of mass m at length L at an angle of theta from the vertical; gravity pulls down with force -mg, balanced by the tension in the string T(theta) and the perpendicular force F(theta) that causes the pendulum to swing](text:image:8878) | <p>**In this script, students will...**</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} implement Euler's method for first-order initial value problems</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} calculate the error of their numerical solution</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} implement a trapezoidal method</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} implement a four-step Runge-Kutta method</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} compare results with the built-in MATLAB solver `ode45`</p> | <p>**Applications**</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} Model a pendulum with increasingly realistic assumptions</p><p></p><p>**Scaffolded Template Scripts**</p><p>[`eulerMethodDE.m`](file:./FunctionLibrary/eulerMethodDE.m)</p><p>[`rk4.m`](file:./FunctionLibrary/rk4.m)</p> |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text:table]
%[text] ### Supporting Scripts
%[text] - [`PendulumModels.m`](file:./Scripts/PendulumModels.m) \
%[text] ### [**NumericalPDEs.m**](file:./Scripts/NumericalPDEs.m) 
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[-1,262,308],"ignoreHeader":true}
%[text] | <p></p><p></p><p>![A numerical solution to the heat equation for a piecewise-linear initial condition](text:image:4e41)</p> | <p>**In this script, students will...**</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} identify errors from discretizing the problem and from discretizing the method and choose appropriate parameters to minimize overall error</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} explain the importance of stability when choosing a numerical method</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} implement explicit, implicit, and Crank-Nicolson methods to solve a 1-D heat equation</p> | <p>**Applications**</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"\*"} Solve a heat equation</p><p></p><p>**Scaffolded Template Scripts**</p><p>[`explicitPDE.m`](file:./FunctionLibrary/explicitPDE.m)</p><p>[`implicitPDE.m`](file:./FunctionLibrary/implicitPDE.m)</p><p>[`cnPDE.m`](file:./FunctionLibrary/cnPDE.m)</p> |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text:table]
